WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- I don't know if this is true,

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:03.600
because this is an odd place to start,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.060
but did you perform in libraries?

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.522
Is this a real thing?
- I did preform in libraries,

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:08.355
yeah.
- (laughing) Like, I feel

00:00:08.355 --> 00:00:10.710
like you're supposed to
be quiet in libraries.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.940
- That's true typically.
- I love you,

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.010
you're always doing something
different than the norm.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.870
- Yeah.
- So how did that,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:16.950
like what?

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:17.880
- It sounds crazy.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.320
- [Kelly] It sounds crazy.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.339
- That's a non-descript.

00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:21.750
- Okay.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.923
- There was an afterschool
program when I was growing up,

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:24.756
- Okay.

00:00:24.756 --> 00:00:28.020
called YOUmedia that was at
the Harold Washington Library

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.640
downtown in Chicago,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.700
and one day I was walking
down the street after school,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.950
and somebody was like, "Hey,
we got a studio in here,"

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.300
and I was like, "That's a library."

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.040
He was like, "It's both,"
(audience laughing)

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.030
and-
- That's cool.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.500
- Yeah, so I came in and checked it out,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.030
and they had free studio time

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.200
for all kids
- What?

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.280
- that were 18 and under.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.290
They had like free production classes,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.490
like, I'm nice with it,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.440
'cause I was literally at the same time

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.340
that I was going to high school,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.660
and not paying attention to class,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.868
I was paying hella attention
in this afterschool program.

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:56.110
- Yeah.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:59.430
- And they had this open mic program there

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.360
called Lyricist Loft

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.090
that was ran by my mentor,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.980
and a lot of people's
mentors outta Chicago,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.190
Vic Mensa, and NO NAME,
and Sava, and Mick,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.750
a lot of people went to this program,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:10.950
like, people around my age,

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.833
that kinda blew up in
hip-hop from Chicago.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.290
Yeah, and we would do
this open mic program,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.390
and he would like, you know,

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.891
show us what we were doing wrong,

00:01:20.891 --> 00:01:22.290
and help us get better as performers.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.220
I did that from when I was like 14

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:26.820
to like 18, really 19.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.829
They had to like, kick
me out of the program,

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:29.662
(audience laughing)
'cause I was trying

00:01:29.662 --> 00:01:30.495
to stay in there.

00:01:30.495 --> 00:01:32.640
- I know I would've
loved that opportunity.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.010
Like, that studio time is expensive.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:35.843
- [Chance] Yeah.

00:01:35.843 --> 00:01:37.541
- Like, that's an incredible opportunity.

00:01:37.541 --> 00:01:38.510
- Yeah, it changed my life.
- It's so cool.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.720
- It changed all of our lives,

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.190
everybody that came there,

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.023
and it was free.
- Yeah.

00:01:42.023 --> 00:01:45.000
- It was just like, you just had to come.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.320
- That is awesome.
- Yeah.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.780
- Just showing up and people
help you out, awesome.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.640
Well, you recently put on a huge festival,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.473
we were talking about it,

00:01:51.473 --> 00:01:52.470
it was in Ghana, right?

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.580
- Yes.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.110
- [Kelly] Tell everybody
why you started this.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.620
- Yeah, so, in January, we just had

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.914
the first successful
Black Star Line Festival.

00:02:01.914 --> 00:02:02.904
- [Kelly] That's so cool. (clapping)

00:02:02.904 --> 00:02:04.230
(audience clapping)
- [Chance] Yeah.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.618
- [Kelly] And it's huge.

00:02:05.618 --> 00:02:07.285
Wasn't it like 50,000 people?

00:02:07.285 --> 00:02:08.118
It's like some crazy number.
- Yeah,

00:02:08.118 --> 00:02:10.510
50,000 people, 52,000 people

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.840
in Accra, Ghana,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.060
that's in West Africa.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.340
Ghana has been like, the stronghold

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.040
of like, Black liberation,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.980
anti-imperialism, Pan-Africanism,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.330
a lot of like, ideals,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.480
that we as a people hold near and dear,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.640
and are a big part of our liberation,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:30.600
have been like,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.730
you know, uplifted by Ghana, historically,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.860
like, since the fifties,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.560
since they got their independence,

00:02:38.132 --> 00:02:39.330
and me and Vic Mensa,

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.320
one of my closest friends,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.910
wanted to put together an event

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.303
that Black people could travel to,

00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:46.200
and, you know, be in
confidence and hang out,

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.760
and be safe and listen to great music.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.243
We had T-Pain, Erykah Badu,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.290
Sarkodie, Jeremih,
myself and Vic performed,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.520
a bunch of awesome people.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.820
Dave Chappelle did a talk down there.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.970
It was like a basically a week
long festival of activities

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.400
that were free for people from abroad,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.420
Black people from abroad,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.270
to come together and kick
it and learn some stuff.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.670
- I love that you became
what someone gave you

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.829
when you were a kid.
- Yeah.

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:13.662
- You know what I'm saying?

00:03:13.662 --> 00:03:14.495
You're walking by and someone said,

00:03:14.495 --> 00:03:16.200
"Hey, you can come do this
and be a part of this,"

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.700
and you're working with all
these different artists,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.380
and it, kind of, is like,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.490
the evolution of that,

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.170
like what you're doing
for all these artists,

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.580
and say, "Hey, come
here, this is like, free.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.590
You can come and have a good
time," and that's awesome.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:26.790
- Yeah, no, totally.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.470
- I don't know if you put
that together, but I just did.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.303
You're welcome.

00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:30.136
- No, I appreciate you

00:03:30.136 --> 00:03:31.318
putting that together.
- Yeah.

00:03:31.318 --> 00:03:32.151
(Kelly laughing)
- No, it's totally what it is.

00:03:32.151 --> 00:03:33.150
I would not be here

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.400
if it wasn't for the programs I went to,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.780
the free things that people did,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.460
or like the mentorships

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.490
that certain men and women
gave me when I was a shorty,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.803
that just made me think a certain way;

00:03:44.973 --> 00:03:47.790
and I feel like that's what we
as a people historically did,

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.450
was we lived in communal
spaces and like, you know,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.090
helped the next people
understand how things work,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:54.960
and, yeah.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.090
- It's a really cool domino effect.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.480
- [Chance] Absolutely.
- I'm just pointing it out.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.313
- You're so wise.

00:03:59.313 --> 00:04:00.840
- Yeah, I'm so wise,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.400
It's really what I am.
- You are, that's what it is.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.130
- So, wait, so you've partnered
with a few artists to make,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.990
this is cool, your album cover art?

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:07.920
- Yes.
- Was this on purpose?

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.090
Like, you went out to find these artists?

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.910
- So, what's been happening is I've been,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.860
since my first trip to Ghana,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.450
I met with,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.340
I stayed with, like a group

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.290
of this collective of artists,

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.940
painters and photographers, and such,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.810
from West Africa,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.610
but from a bunch of different countries,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.100
and really just got to like, you know,

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.000
getting to know them,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.820
and getting to understand
how their, you know,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.050
how their field works,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.320
and realize there's a lot of similarities,

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.750
overlapping of like, the exploitation

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.240
of, you know, music artists,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.801
and the exploitation of visual artists.

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:44.490
- Yeah.
- And through that, like,

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.780
we just had a conversation about

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.601
how to create more community
and collaborate together.

00:04:48.601 --> 00:04:49.440
- [Kelly] Better network.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.373
- Yeah, so now,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.940
my next project is called
"Star Line Gallery."

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.240
It's a collaboration with
16 different visual artists,

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.530
fine artists from around the world,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.630
Black folks that I met through my travels,

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.960
where we got together, and
I went through my medium,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.860
they went through their medium,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.300
and we came together to make a piece.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.090
So, so far I've released four of them.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.590
They're in the-
- That's so cool.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.750
- Major institutions,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.160
the MOCA in Los Angeles,

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.190
The Museum of Contemporary
Arts in Chicago,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.440
Art Basel, Switzerland.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.387
Those are some of the things.

00:05:20.387 --> 00:05:21.220
- That's so cool!

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.160
- Yeah, so that's Mía Lee,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.960
is an amazing painter from Chicago,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.220
she did "Yah Know."

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.950
Naïla Opiangah, she
painted "Child of God."

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:29.783
Nikko Washington.
- Who did this one up here?

00:05:29.783 --> 00:05:31.800
- That one up top is,
Yannis Davy Guibinga,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.190
he's an amazing photographer
from Gabon, Africa,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.570
and he lives in Toronto.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.220
But, he's phenomenal.
- That is a cool shot.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.290
- Yeah, that's the thing,

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.330
it's like, as artists,
we're communicators,

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:43.860
high-level communicators,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.440
and we have these conversations

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.180
that we need to get out to the world,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.470
and sometimes it takes like,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.390
collaboration between two artists

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:52.950
to really like, get across the point.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.480
So like, when you get a chance,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.770
check out that one that you like,

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:56.730
that's "The Highs and The Lows."

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:57.780
Check out "The Highs and The Lows."

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.530
- Of course, I like that one, sure.

